WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.830
Picture a café that writes every order in a notebook.

2
00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:09.668
When a notebook fills, the owner shelves it and starts a new one.

3
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:14.804
Each Monday, she throws out every notebook whose last order is over a week old.

4
00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:21.641
Kafka, the program that keeps logs of records for other programs to read, has the owner's problem.

5
00:00:21.641 --> 00:00:22.781
A log only grows.

6
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:28.639
At 3,000 payments a second, about 1 KB each, that's 3 MB a second.

7
00:00:28.639 --> 00:00:31.866
About 260 GB a day, and 1.8 TB a week.

8
00:00:31.866 --> 00:00:36.707
How do you delete the front of something that only grows?

9
00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:42.925
The simplest idea: keep the log in one big file, and cut the oldest part off the front.

10
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:44.250
Here's the catch.

11
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:51.706
A file system, the part of the computer that keeps files on disk, can cut a file short at its end for almost nothing.

12
00:00:51.706 --> 00:00:53.363
It can't cut at the start.

13
00:00:53.633 --> 00:01:02.988
So to drop the oldest 260 GB from a 1.8 TB file, you'd copy the other 1.5 TB into a new file.

14
00:01:02.988 --> 00:01:03.993
Every day.

15
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:10.293
That's tearing out page one of a notebook by copying every other page into a new notebook.

16
00:01:10.293 --> 00:01:10.831
By hand.

17
00:01:10.831 --> 00:01:11.774
Every morning.

18
00:01:11.774 --> 00:01:15.140
So deleting has to mean throwing away whole files.

19
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:18.829
The café already solved this: many notebooks, not one.

20
00:01:18.829 --> 00:01:20.905
Only the newest takes new orders.

21
00:01:20.905 --> 00:01:25.181
When it's full, it goes on the shelf, and nobody writes in it again.

22
00:01:25.433 --> 00:01:30.261
And throwing out the oldest costs one trip to the bin, however thick the notebook is.

23
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:31.850
Kafka does the same.

24
00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:34.616
Each notebook is a file called a segment.

25
00:01:34.616 --> 00:01:37.923
The one taking new records is the active segment.

26
00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:42.057
Closing a full one and opening a new one is called rolling.

27
00:01:42.057 --> 00:01:44.100
A closed segment never changes.

28
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:49.045
And deleting old data means deleting old files, which costs almost nothing.

29
00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:58.611
Here's a small log from the lesson's lab: 400 records of 10 KB each, in segments of 1 MiB, a little more than a megabyte.

30
00:01:58.611 --> 00:02:00.928
About 104 records fit in each.

31
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:10.723
Each segment is named after the offset of its first record, its base offset, padded with zeros to 20 digits.

32
00:02:10.723 --> 00:02:16.189
So: 0, 104, 208, and the active one, 312, holding the last 88.

33
00:02:16.433 --> 00:02:19.040
Now a reader asks for offset 230.

34
00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.568
Which file does the broker open?

35
00:02:21.568 --> 00:02:22.832
Pause and guess.

36
00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:26.455
208.

37
00:02:26.455 --> 00:02:35.375
The broker keeps its files sorted by base offset, and picks the largest one at or below 230.

38
00:02:35.375 --> 00:02:36.927
312 is too high.

39
00:02:36.927 --> 00:02:38.381
208 is the one.

40
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.498
But a segment can be 1 GiB.

41
00:02:40.498 --> 00:02:46.123
Reading it from the start, at 2 GB a second, takes half a second for one lookup.

42
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.441
So next to each .log file sits a .index file.

43
00:02:49.441 --> 00:02:55.117
It maps offsets to byte positions in the file, like the index at the back of a book.

44
00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:03.556
It's sparse: an entry about every 4,096 bytes, not one per record, so it's small enough to keep in memory.

45
00:03:03.833 --> 00:03:11.696
The broker looks up 230 in the index, jumps to the nearest entry at or below it, and reads forward a few kilobytes.

46
00:03:11.696 --> 00:03:15.388
A third file, the time index, does the same for times.

47
00:03:15.633 --> 00:03:19.418
Two settings decide when the active segment rolls.

48
00:03:19.418 --> 00:03:24.564
segment.bytes, 1 GiB by default: roll before the file grows past it.

49
00:03:24.833 --> 00:03:31.176
segment.ms, 7 days: roll if the segment is older than that when a new record arrives.

50
00:03:31.467 --> 00:03:32.367
And the catch.

51
00:03:32.367 --> 00:03:34.424
Deleting means whole files only.

52
00:03:34.424 --> 00:03:37.446
Remember the owner who keeps orders for a week?

53
00:03:37.446 --> 00:03:41.883
A notebook that also holds last Thursday's orders stays on the shelf.

54
00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:47.224
So an inspector can find a twelve-day-old order there, and the rule wasn't broken.

55
00:03:47.224 --> 00:03:51.098
Kafka keeps records the same way: often longer than you asked.

56
00:03:51.098 --> 00:03:52.723
The next section shows it.

57
00:03:52.967 --> 00:03:54.950
So: one log, cut into files.

58
00:03:54.950 --> 00:04:03.519
New records go only into the active one, closed ones never change, and deleting old data means throwing away whole files.

59
00:04:03.733 --> 00:04:08.753
The file names find the right file, and a small index finds the place inside it.

60
00:04:09.233 --> 00:04:10.500
One quick check.

61
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.797
A reader asks for offset 103.

62
00:04:12.797 --> 00:04:15.332
Which file does the broker open?

63
00:04:15.332 --> 00:04:16.599
Pause and think.

64
00:04:19.833 --> 00:04:21.159
The one named 0.

65
00:04:21.159 --> 00:04:28.700
The next file starts at 104, one record later, so 103 is the last record of the first file.
